<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juba II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 48 BCE–<a href="/years/23">23 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Juba II of Mauretania</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juba II was the son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-i">Juba I of Numidia</a>, raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> after his father's defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>. Augustus made him client king of Mauretania in 25 BCE, and he married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-selene-ii">Cleopatra Selene</a>, daughter of Cleopatra and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a>. A learned writer, he ruled from Caesarea (modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherchell">Cherchell</a>), which later became the capital of a Roman province.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juba-ii.jpg" alt="Juba II" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marie-Lan Nguyen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Juba_II_of_Mauretania_Ny_Carlsberg_Glyptotek_IN1591.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the Battle of Thapsus in 46 BCE Caesar defeated them, and <b>Juba</b>, refused entry to his own town of Zama, died in a suicide pact with the Roman general Marcus Petreius.</blockquote><a href="/empire/numidia">Numidia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 6 CE they rebelled against <b>Juba II</b> and were defeated by Cossus Cornelius Lentulus, who took the title Gaetulicus.</blockquote><a href="/state/gaetuli">Gaetuli</a></li><li><blockquote>After Bocchus II bequeathed the kingdom to Rome, Augustus installed <b>Juba II</b> in 25 BCE, and Juba married Cleopatra Selene, daughter of Cleopatra VII.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-mauretania">Kingdom of Mauretania</a></li><li><blockquote>After the fall of Carthage Lixus came under the Kingdom of Mauretania, minting coins with Punic legends, and was fully absorbed under <b>Juba II</b> around 25 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/lixus">Lixus</a></li><li><blockquote>Its capital was Caesarea (Cherchell), the former royal city of <b>Juba II</b>, and it was governed by an equestrian procurator with auxiliary troops.</blockquote><a href="/state/mauretania-caesariensis">Mauretania Caesariensis</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippo-regius">Hippo Regius</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a>. Died in Tipasa in Mauretania, in what is now <a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.88,7.75&amp;year=-2" aria-label="Hippo Regius on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4744 835 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Juba II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5194 1105h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5194 1105h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.88,7.75&amp;year=-2">What was here in 2 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
